description:
A **least-recently-used** (LRU) cache keeps a limited number of entries; when it is full, adding a new one evicts the entry that was used longest ago. Web browsers, databases and operating systems all use one. Every operation must be **O(1)**, which needs two structures working together:

- a `HashMap` from key to node, for finding an entry in O(1);
- a **doubly linked list** of nodes in order of use, most recent at the front, for moving and evicting in O(1).

Write the list yourself (no `LinkedHashMap`, `LinkedList` or `ArrayDeque`):

```java
// LruCache.java
class LruCache<K, V> {
    LruCache(int capacity)                       // capacity >= 1, else IllegalArgumentException
    V get(K key)                                 // null when absent; a hit makes the entry the most recent
    void put(K key, V value)                     // adds or replaces (and makes it the most recent); evicts when full
    boolean remove(K key)
    int size()
    List<K> keys()                               // most recent first
    void onEvict(BiConsumer<K, V> listener)      // called with each evicted entry (not for remove or replace)
    Stats stats()
}

// Stats.java
record Stats(long hits, long misses, long evictions) {
    double hitRate()                             // hits / (hits + misses), or 0 when there were no gets
}
```

